age and it's also at the end  of the day just treating the problem not  the source of the problem  so there's been successful research in  other humid climates that have this  excessive growth problem in new zealand  long island and virginia that have shown  that when you replace using herbicides  direct lander vines and instead plant a  very intentionally planted under vine  cover crop not only are you eliminating  herbicide use but you're actually  getting a positive deviating effect  however as this californian can tell you  the finger lakes is pretty cold  and we aren't as warm as these other  regions so we have sort of a different  management  practicality to work around in the  winter we mound up a lot of soil around  the graft union of vines the european  grapevines you guys are going to be  familiar with including riesling which  is what my research is done on are all  planted on rootstock so if we ever had a  really hard freeze these vines would  kill to the ground and all you're going  to be left with is your rootstock which  isn't going to produce your fruit and  you're pretty much back to square one so  by healing up this dirt all over those  graft unions you're ensuring the  survival of some scion buds which come  the spring you heal back down you can  grow up  your your riesling or your cab franc or  whatever it may be from those surviving  buds and you're not back to square one  you still have some work ahead of you  but you're not devastated also at the  end of the day crop insurance requires  growers to heal up so regardless of  whether you're going to take the chance  or not you're pretty much going to do it  so given this  need to heal up vines in the winter and  heal them back down in the spring we  have to operate on kind of a different  system vineyard floor management system  than these other regions which showed  success with looking at permanently  established perennials  that's why my objective for my master's  work was to look at annually 