 and this is sort of  would be the end date then for my  undervine cover crops because we have a  giant cultivation operation underneath  the vines at this point the vines have  pretty much gone dormant and have leaves  drop and they're preparing to hibernate  in the winter like i wish i was  as i said i do make wine from the juice  this is sort of a standard white wine  fermentation procedure  that in general was held for both of my  lots what's important to note here is  that for each treatment there are  duplicate lots of wine made  and the whole idea is since this is a  viticultural trial i'm trying to be as  hands-off as possible in the enological  process and try to allow characteristics  in the vineyard to be carried through so  i'm using a yeast that doesn't throw off  a lot of interesting aromas i uh  chopalize or add sugar to a standard  sugar level i supplement the yeast or  the yam level to make sure  these yeast are going to be able to go  and i do ferment these wines to dryness  so for the multi-dimensional sorting  analysis by wine aroma the big fancy  word for my sensory tests in short  all the duplicate lots were presented to  panelists and they were asked to sort  wines by similarity into groups if you  found a wine to be similar  to another wine you put them in the same  group if you found them to be different  you put them in different groups this  gets a lot of grouping data data that's  then run through sas and can give you a  visual representation of the differences  which i'll be showing you later so i had  two field sites that i did my research  on the first was an uh commercial  vineyard on the east side of seneca lake  um these vines were older almost 20 year  old 20 years old now  i planted on a honey oil silt loam  riesling and what i want to note here is  it's a scott henry training system  meaning that there is both an upper and  lower fruiting zone  this is important because we'll often do  this in vineyards that have sort of  excessive growth but this already has  been done at this vineyard and we were  still seeing excessive growth  um there were treatments the underlying  cover crops were originally planted in  2011 and carried through through this  year in a randomized complete block  design with four replicates per  treatment  my four treatments were as such annual  rye grass planted in the middle of may  this is a later establishing while  planted in the middle of may it doesn't  really get going and i noticed the most  biomass accumulation much later in the  season such as july august and it grows  well through october  buckwheat that beautiful white flowering  plant  has seeded in late may interesting about  buckwheat is it frost kills so that'd be  ideal for a kind of abbreviated time  frame that we're looking for  buckwheat also has a lot of notoriety  right now because work out of berkeley  and work by greg globe here has shown  that it could potentially increase  the incidence of generalist predators in  the vineyard and increase rates of  parasitism of vineyard pests there's a  lot of interest around buckwheat  natural vegetation polite word for  wheats  we allow them to grow throughout the  season whatever may be there and then  our control is the traditional  glyphosate spray  applied one to two times a year the  underlying cover crop treatments were  mowed as needed to keep them from  getting too tall into the canopy and  that ended up being one to two times a  year  my second site is the cornell orchards  at lansing in one of the riesling blocks  that i kind of inherited from ian  merwin's old work  here these fines were originally planted  in 2007 it once again is a two-tier flat  bow which is a double canopy fruiting  system and that picture that i used on  the slide about excessive vigor came  from this site so i can assure you that  there's a lot of excess vigor here  these treatments were originally  established in 2012 and carried out this  year this site's a little compli