ere i  will be going off to savion blanc  country for quite a while where of all  things i'll be doing some undervine  cover crop research  and i've also accepted um  a lab tech position with individual wine  company  to work the 2014 harvest over there but  never fear i'll be back  um i've just i've accepted uh continuing  on for my phd working here with justine  to stop or to continue on with some of  the work that um i've done here and  presented to you today to you for my  master's at this time i'd be happy to  take any questions and thank you  everyone for listening  uh definitely so the question was in  relation to the fact that uh the annual  rye grass treatment exhibited higher  levels of phosphorus and um iron and had  had certain yield effects  well yes it was it was not a predominant  effect the number of berries per cluster  was significant but not on a level that  would uh i would have been shot from the  rooftops  and it wasn't consistently it was only  the one year that we saw a reduction in  cluster size so  um  my sort of response is that yes we  certainly think the soil microbiota and  all that could be dramatically affecting  it the soil side of the undervine cover  crops is more the project of um it was  becky ciriani and adam carl in our lab  has taken over and looking a lot at that  and that's sort of it but definitely  it's something to consider i think  what's unique in this situation that  could be dramatically affecting the soil  biota is the fact that we have hilling  up so we're essentially doing a giant  mulching of these  decomposing or cover crops into the soil  every winter and then we're mixing it  all up again in the spring so i think  that's potentially where you're getting  some of these interesting effects and  how these plants are decomposing too  could have some dramatic effects so  certainly it's of something of interest  and yeah those results were a little  surprising and i'm still waiting to get  results back from our cornell nutrient  lab and when i get those i'll be able to  further see if this is a consistent  year-to-year effect  the questions about training of the  sensory individuals in short they  weren't so for this sort of study you do  not want trained individuals they are  given an explicit set of instructions  they are allowed to ask me questions and  i can  expound on the procedure but the idea is  that i am looking for  a just normal white wine consumer the  one limitation was that on average you  drink white wine about once a month i  was looking for wine drinkers not just  anybody but um we weren't this wasn't  what was what you might be thinking of  which is called a descriptive analysis  panel where you have explicit training  on the different characteristics um  you'll have training on what you think  the characteristics are in this type of  wine and then subsequently the standards  of lemon flavor or standards of  acid flavor and those sorts of things  this was sort of not supposed to be that  this is about your own perception of the  aroma i'm not looking to identify aromas  so however you perceive them to be  different you group them accordingly  so the question is is there a tendency  in difference testing to find more  differences that may exist that's always  the risk when you're asking someone to  find differences they can be prone to  that  this is a little more i would say  they are presented with the option of  putting them all in one group which  people did  if they found that there were no  differences so while it is always a  problem there's always a problem like  that i think with almost any sensory  test you do there's going to be some  bias that way so i think this test was  pretty representative of being able to  determine differences but like i said at  the end of the day all i can say is  differences i can't tell if they were  preferred or not or while relatively  they might have been different from each  other but if overall compared to all the  