gement wise it wasn't  pretty and had offered a little few more  difficulties at the end of the day  though that was about as minimal  maintenance as i could and it was still  a pretty well established system so i  say to grow up what i would say from  growers is that it's unlikely you're  going to be seeing dramatic effects why  not try it and see what works for your  system  yeah the questions are growers ready to  change practices the world of  viticulture is notorious for it's like  the slowest rate of adoption curve ever  in terms of any agricultural field  we are based in history right rules and  how we grow things how we make our wine  that's you know literally  law on how you make your wine in europe  um  our growers ready to change i think some  are and there's always early adopters  who benefit from this kind of thing  there's definitely one grower in this  region who plants buckwheat not only as  an inter-row cover crop but just  everywhere under vines in a rows  everywhere he's willing to try it you  know you'll always have those people who  are  and i think it's stepping away a bit of  that perception of that very nice  looking vineyard of having vegetation  free  and stepping more into maybe more of the  agroman ecosystem management perspective  at the end of the day vineyards are also  places where people host weddings and  those sorts of things and it's always  hard for me to remove that when i'm at  the end of the day asking a grower to  make do something a little different  the chicory  yeah  good question um  i think even though the resulting vine  water status isn't being isn't that  different because these are own root or  not own rooted sorry but these are  vines that while grown with some  irrigation  i still think these roots are going  incredibly deep as we can see off in the  finger lakes and i think maybe while the  whole vine water status isn't being that  affected they are having to rely on  drawing water from those deeper roots  than the upper surface of the roots  where the  cover crop is actively growing and i  think there might be some of that just  physiological differences that are  causing some of that  similarly uh  it is known  that pre-verizon water deficits will  significantly impact fruit set that's  mark matthews work and um i think maybe  there were just some differences but  just so slight that statistically they  weren't there but um i yeah i don't  really have a great question or answer  to that unfortunately but i think it has  a lot to do with just the sensitivity of  my measurements  and maybe some physiological processes  going on in the vine that i'm not able  to detect  sure  i establish my cover crops by hand  seeding  and hand cultivating over them um it's  not that glorious but uh it was  efficient they sufficiently emerged  every year um we there is if this was to  be adopted on a larger scale we  understand most growers are not going to  be willing to walk over their acres and  acres of vineyards with hand seating but  uh there's interest and potential with a  few of our collaborators and the  extension side of things that have been  coming up with different ideas of how to  implement this on a vineyard level scale  but yeah i know that's right now is just  just just me myself and i have labor of  hand seating  yeah so the questions about the site to  site differences which there certainly  was  definitely i think there were  differences by the nature of the age of  the vines as i said that first sight was  almost 20 year old vines and the second  site here these vines are younger and um  i think it's just a little less fertile  a little less fertile a little less deep  soil of a site um what is interesting to  know though the one  carryover cover crop of buckwheat pretty  much had no significant effects at  either side so that's sort of my take  home message there's going to be more  and less vigorous sites here in the  finger lakes we have vineyards planted  on the who